            .speedResponsiveness: "response",
            .unitKbps: "Kbps",
            .docSpeedFull: "measures your connection with macOS's built-in networkQuality: traffic goes to Apple's CDN servers, no third-party services involved.\n\n• reading it — ↓ download and ↑ upload.\n\n• response — how many round trips the network completes per minute under full load: below 100 calls and streams will suffer, 800+ is great. shown in the result's tooltip.\n\n• why live numbers move — the test ramps up parallel streams and loads the link both ways, so live figures float; the final number is the settled capacity. it's usually below speedtest.net peaks — those measure an idealized link to your ISP's own server.\n\n• staleness — a result older than 30 minutes (or taken on another Wi-Fi) turns dim: time to re-measure.\n\n• download in practice — 10 Mbps is enough for browsing and music, 25 for HD video, 50+ for 4K and quick app downloads, 100+ for heavy files without waiting.\n\n• upload in practice — 5 Mbps carries a call with screen sharing, 10+ HD video calls and cloud backups, 25+ comfortable streaming and big uploads.\n\n• low RPM on a fast line — RPM is delay under load, not speed. «under load» means the line is busy with something else: a file downloading, a cloud backup, someone at home streaming. in those moments router queues (bufferbloat) hold packets. 0–100 — on a busy line, voices in calls drop out and sound distorted, pages take seconds to open; 100–400 — only short hiccups under load; 400–800 — load is barely noticeable; 800+ — calls stay smooth even next to torrents. if you rarely load the line, low RPM won't bother you; the cure is a router with smart queue management (SQM).",
            .torrentLabel: "torrents", .torrentEnable: "enable torrents",
            .torrentGetting: "getting engine", .torrentVerifying: "verifying",
            .torrentDrop: "drop a .torrent or paste a magnet", .torrentPaste: "paste magnet",
            .torrentPeers: "peers", .torrentEta: "eta", .torrentRatio: "ratio", .torrentReveal: "reveal in Finder",
            .torrentRemoveConfirm: "delete files too?", .torrentRemoveKeep: "keep files", .torrentRemoveDelete: "delete with files", .torrentRemoveTorrent: "delete torrent", .torrentRemoveMagnet: "delete magnet", .torrentFilesRemoved: "files removed · resume to re-download",
            .torrentFolderLabel: "download folder", .torrentStopRatio: "stop seeding when uploaded = downloaded",
            .torrentRateLabel: "speed limit", .torrentAdvancedInfo: "advanced info",
            .torrentRateHint: "0 — no limit",
            .torrentMakeDefault: "make Hop default for .torrent + magnet", .torrentReadFailed: "couldn't read this torrent",
            .torrentAdd: "add torrent", .torrentRemove: "remove", .torrentShowWhenEmpty: "show add card when empty",
            .torrentAddHint: "add a .torrent file, or a magnet from the clipboard",
            .torrentDefaultAsk: "Open .torrent files in Hop by default?", .torrentDefaultDo: "make default", .torrentDefaultLater: "not now",
            .torrentAll: "select all", .torrentNone: "deselect all",.torrentFilesSummary: "%1$@ of %2$@ files",
            .torrentFetching: "fetching file list…", .torrentNeedsFree: "needs %1$@ · %2$@ free", .torrentDownload: "download",
            .featureNewBadge: "new", .featureEnable: "enable", .featureHide: "hide",
            .featureTorrentTitle: "torrents", .featureTorrentBody: "download .torrent files and magnet links right in Hop", .torrentEngineNote: "saying yes downloads the engine right away (~26 MB)",
            .colorLabel: "color", .colorPick: "pick", .colorPicking: "picking…",
            .colorEmpty: "no colors yet", .hkColor: "eyedropper",
            .docColorFull: "the eyedropper for colors on screen: press the shortcut (or the pick button) and macOS's own loupe appears — click any pixel and that color lands in your clipboard history, already copied and ready to paste.\n\n• {sym:eyedropper} picking — the loupe magnifies whatever is under the cursor, so a single pixel is easy to hit. escape cancels and copies nothing.\n\n• notation — hex, rgb or hsl. switching it also rewrites the newest color, so you can pick once and paste in whichever form you need.\n\n• recent colors — the swatch row keeps the last six; click one to copy it again in the current notation. they sit in the clipboard history too, searchable like any other entry.\n\n• no screen recording — the system loupe hands back one color and nothing else; Hop never sees a picture of your screen. that permission belongs to text recognition, a separate module.\n\n• the module ships hidden — it is made for designers and developers. turn it on in settings → modules & tabs.",
            .ocrLabel: "screen text", .ocrRead: "read", .ocrHint: "text or qr code",
            .ocrSelecting: "select an area…", .ocrReading: "reading…", .ocrCopied: "copied",
            .ocrNothing: "nothing to read", .ocrNeedsPermission: "needs screen recording", .ocrOpenSettings: "open settings",
            .ocrFailed: "didn't work",
            .docOcrFull: "text and qr codes off the screen: press the shortcut (or the read button), frame any area with macOS's own crosshair, and whatever is inside lands in your clipboard history — already copied, with no screenshot left on your desktop.\n\n• {sym:text.viewfinder} what it reads — printed and rendered text of any kind: a paused video, a protected pdf, a dialog that won't let you select, a frame from a game. qr codes and barcodes are read in the same pass, and a framed code wins over the caption printed beside it.\n\n• where it goes — into the clipboard history as an ordinary entry, so you can find it later by searching. that link is the whole point: live text and the standalone grabbers hand you the text once and forget it.\n\n• line breaks are kept — a table or a code snippet stays readable instead of collapsing into one run-on line.\n\n• recognition is Apple's own Vision, entirely on this Mac and offline; nothing is uploaded anywhere. it reads your app language plus english.\n\n• screen recording — macOS asks for it once, because framing an area means reading pixels; without it the capture would come back black. Hop reads only the rectangle you draw, only when you ask. escape cancels and copies nothing.\n\n• the module ships hidden — turn it on in settings → modules & tabs.",
            .archiveLabel: "archive", .archiveDrop: "drop an archive to unpack", .archiveDropHint: "files or folders — packed instead",
            .archiveUnpacking: "unpacking…", .archivePacking: "packing…", .archiveUnpacked: "unpacked",
            .archivePacked: "packed", .archiveGettingHelper: "getting helper", .archiveHelperFailed: "helper unavailable",
            .archiveEmpty: "nothing inside", .archiveFailed: "didn't work",
            .docArchiveFull: "packing and unpacking by drag & drop, with the result always NEXT TO the original — no window, no wizard, no downloads folder to dig through.\n\n• {sym:archivebox} unpacking — drop an archive onto the card and it opens right where it lives. one item inside comes out as that item; several come out inside one folder named after the archive, so nothing is ever scattered across your desktop. an existing name is never overwritten — the new one is numbered, the way Finder does it.\n\n• packing — drop files or folders instead and they become one archive beside them. the format chips choose what to make: zip, tar.gz or 7z.\n\n• what opens natively — zip, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz and gz work straight away with tools macOS already has.\n\n• rar and 7z — these need the 7-Zip helper (~6 MB), fetched once the first time you drop one and installed only after its signature checks out. Hop unpacks rar but never creates it: the format is proprietary and no free packer may write it.\n\n• archives made here stay clean — no __MACOSX leftovers, so they look right when opened on Windows or Linux.",
            .convCanDocuments: "documents", .convModeLabel: "mode", .convModeCompress: "compress",
            .convDocNote: "word layout like columns and footnotes is simplified; pdf → md extracts text with headings, not layout",
            .docConverterDocs: "documents — markdown, word files and pdfs turned into one another, in the same window and the same batch.\n\n• md → pdf — laid out by Hop itself: headings, lists, quotes, code blocks and links, on A4 pages with proper margins. the markdown engine is ours, so the result looks deliberate rather than like a browser printout.\n\n• word (.docx, .doc, .rtf) → pdf or md — text, headings, emphasis, lists, simple tables and images come across. columns, footnotes and headers do not: those are layout, and the conversion is honest about losing them.\n\n• pdf → md — switch the pdf group to md. a pdf stores layout rather than structure, so this is text EXTRACTION with heading guesses: a text-first document comes out well, a magazine spread comes out as prose. a scanned page with no text layer is read with Vision, the same recognition the screen-text module uses.\n\n• md → docx — for when a document has to arrive in a shape Word will open.\n\n• everything is native and offline: no converter service, no bundled office suite, nothing to download.",
            .keylockLabel: "keyboard", .keylockStart: "lock", .keylockHint: "for wiping — mouse unlocks",
            .keylockSecondsUnit: "s", .keylockTitle: "keyboard locked",
            .keylockBody: "wipe it — the keys do nothing.\nunlock with the mouse, or wait for the timer.",
            .keylockDone: "done",
            .docKeylockFull: "cleaning mode: one button and the whole keyboard stops responding, so you can wipe it without shutting the Mac down or closing the lid.\n\n• {sym:keyboard} what happens — a full-screen cover appears and every key press, release and modifier is swallowed, including the brightness, volume and media row. nothing is recorded: the keystrokes are dropped, never read.\n\n• getting out — the mouse or trackpad: the done button on the cover. a keyboard shortcut would be the one thing that is switched off. the timer (30 seconds, 1 minute or 5 minutes) lets go on its own as a safety net.\n\n• the power button and Touch ID keep working — macOS reserves those, and taking them away would be wrong anyway.\n\n• accessibility permission — the same one the window manager uses, because swallowing keys system-wide is exactly what it governs. without it the keys would keep firing, so Hop asks before promising a lock.",
            .featureToolsTitle: "screen tools",
            .featureToolsBody: "an eyedropper for any color on screen, and text or qr codes read straight into your clipboard history. made for designers and developers, so they stay off unless you want them.",
            .permTab: "permissions", .permGranted: "granted", .permNotGranted: "not granted",
            .permWhenUsed: "asked when used", .permNetworkTitle: "network — antonshakirov.com",
            .permNetworkBody: "checking for updates and downloading them, plus the two optional helpers: the torrent engine and the 7-Zip archiver. that is the only server Hop talks to on its own.",
            .permTorrentTitle: "network — torrent traffic",
            .permTorrentBody: "only with the torrent module on: connections to other peers, exactly like any torrent client. nothing passes through us.",
            .permSpeedTitle: "network — speed test",
            .permSpeedBody: "the speed test runs macOS's own networkQuality tool, which measures against Apple's servers. no third-party service is involved.",
            .permAccessibilityTitle: "accessibility",
            .permAccessibilityBody: "three things need it: pasting into the app underneath (the clipboard's paste button), moving and resizing windows, and the keyboard lock. macOS governs all of them with this one switch.",
            .permScreenTitle: "screen recording",
            .permScreenBody: "only the screen-text module: framing an area means reading its pixels. Hop reads the rectangle you draw, when you ask for it, and nothing else. the eyedropper does NOT need this — the system loupe hands back a single color.",
            .permNotifyTitle: "notifications",
            .permNotifyBody: "the timer's alert when it finishes, and a note when a torrent completes. nothing else is ever posted.",
            .permAdminTitle: "administrator password",
            .permAdminBody: "asked once, by macOS itself, only for the closed-lid mode: keeping the Mac awake with the lid shut needs pmset, which is root-only. no background daemon is installed.",
            .permLoginTitle: "launch at login",
            .permLoginBody: "off unless you turn it on, in settings. it registers Hop with macOS's own login-items service — no scripts, no plists left behind.",
            .permNeverTitle: "what Hop never does",
            .permNeverBody: "no analytics, no telemetry, no account, no crash reports sent anywhere. the clipboard history, the tracker and the to-do list live only on this Mac. files are touched only when you drop them on Hop or pick them yourself, and no permission is requested until the feature that needs it is actually used.",
            .featureShowHere: "show in the panel", .featureSave: "save",
            .trackerLabel: "time tracker", .trackerNewTask: "new task",
            .trackerDelete: "delete",
            .trackerEditHint: "drag or click to adjust the total",
            .trackerLongRun: "still tracking — over 8 hours. forgot to stop?",
            .trackerOverrunBanner: "a task timer has been running for over 8 hours", .trackerOverrunDismiss: "ok",
            .todosLabel: "to-dos", .todosNew: "new task",
            .visibleRowsLabel: "visible rows",
            .aboutTabTasks: "tasks & time",
            .docTrackerFull: "time tracker — a flat list of tasks, each with an all-time total that ticks up while the task runs. only one task runs at a time.\n\n• start / stop — the round button on the left starts a task; starting another stops the first. the total climbs live while a task is active.\n\n• rename — double-click a task's name to change it.\n\n• edit the time — click the total to type it (H:MM:SS, H:MM, or just minutes), or drag across it to scrub ±1 minute per step; the change is saved as a single correction.\n\n• forgot to stop? — once a task has run for over 8 hours, a gentle still-tracking row appears with its own stop button.\n\n• reorder — drag a row up or down to reorder the list.\n\n• {sym:xmark} delete — hover a row to reveal the ✕, click it, then confirm.\n\n• visible rows — in settings, cap the list to 3…15 rows (default 10); above the cap it scrolls, and the 8-hour warning stays pinned in view.",
            .docTodosFull: "to-dos — a simple checklist.\n\n• add — click the + new task row, type, and press return.\n\n• check off — click the circle to complete a task; completed tasks sink to the bottom, dimmed and struck through. unchecking a task returns it to its place among the active ones.\n\n• reorder — drag a row to reorder; active and completed tasks each reorder within their own group.\n\n• {sym:xmark} delete — hover a row to reveal the ✕, click it, then confirm.\n\n• visible rows — in settings, cap the list to 3…15 rows (default 10); above the cap it scrolls, with the add row always in view.",
            .donateTitle: "support hop",
            .donateBody: "your support will help ship new features and keep making hop better",
            .coloredIndicators: "colored indicators",
            .trackerBarTime: "show task time in menu bar",
            .settingsTabLayout: "modules & tabs",
            .modulesInactive: "inactive", .tabDeleteConfirm: "delete this tab? its modules become inactive",
